## 1. Is there a browser in this sandbox?

  $ for b in google-chrome chromium chromium-browser firefox; do which $b; done
  (nothing)

  $ ls /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu | grep -E "lib(nss3|nssutil3|nspr4|gtk-3|gbm)"
  (nothing: no NSS, no NSPR, no GTK, no GBM)

  $ curl -m5 https://registry.npmjs.org/            -> 200
  $ curl -m5 https://storage.googleapis.com/        -> 000 (no route)
  $ curl -m5 https://deb.debian.org/                -> 000 (no route)

Chromium's own download host and apt are unreachable, so a distro browser
cannot be installed. The one reachable source of a Chromium binary is an npm
tarball, so that was tested rather than assumed:

  $ npm install @sparticuz/chromium puppeteer-core      (43 packages, 67 MB)
  $ node -e "chromium.executablePath().then(console.log)"
  /tmp/chromium                                          (209 MB, Chromium 153.0.8010.0)

It does not start: the binary needs libnspr4/libnss3/libnssutil3, which the
image lacks. Postponing symbol resolution with LD_BIND_NOW=0 buys nothing
because the binary is linked -z now:

  $ LD_BIND_NOW=0 /tmp/chromium --version
  /tmp/chromium: error while loading shared libraries: libnspr4.so

A minimal stand-in library was built to get past the loader and find out
whether the rest of the runtime works (57 symbols, generated from the binary's
own undefined-symbol table, returning zero):

  $ gcc -shared -fPIC -Wl,--version-script=stub.map -o libnss3.so stub.c
  $ LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<stub> /tmp/chromium --version
  Chromium 153.0.8010.0

Every page load then dies before rendering anything:

  $ LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<stub> /tmp/chromium --headless=new --no-sandbox --no-zygote \
        --single-process --disable-gpu --dump-dom 'data:text/html,<p>hi</p>'
  Segmentation fault (exit 139), empty stdout
  (same for --headless=old, --in-process-gpu, swiftshader, --disable-gpu,
   --screenshot, --user-data-dir variants: four configurations, four SIGSEGV)

Conclusion: no browser here can execute a page. No browser screenshot is
claimed anywhere in this report.

## 2. What the pixel evidence is, then

The page is driven in jsdom: the real app.js, model.js, stylesheet and real
served data. Each capture asks the page for its own "Export view SVG" (the same
button a user presses), and @resvg/resvg-js rasterises that SVG. Geometry,
colours, shapes, label positions and link layout in the PNGs are therefore the
page's own output; the SVG next to each PNG can be opened in a browser to check
the same thing. HTML chrome (sidebar, toolbar, legend box, timeline) is not in
the images — jsdom has no layout engine — and is covered by the 56 DOM
assertions plus capture.json, which records the badge, legend and timeline text
at each capture.

  $ NODE_PATH=<jsdom> VIS_BASE=http://127.0.0.1:8801 \
      node visualiser/tests/pixel_capture.js \
      --out verification/visualiser-round2 --session round2-residency-20261103-143947
  {"ok": true, "captures": 7, "failed": 0, "errors": []}

  $ NODE_PATH=<resvg> node visualiser/tests/rasterize.js verification/visualiser-round2 1440
  {"rendered": 7, "width": 1440}

