## 3. Visual defects found by looking at the rendered output

Each of these was visible in a rendered PNG, not inferred from code.

(a) The exported view was black-on-black: every mark invisible except amber
    diamonds. Cause: the export copied the stylesheet out of
    document.styleSheets/cssRules, which yields nothing in a non-browser DOM,
    so the file had no colours at all. Fixed by fetching the stylesheet text and
    inlining it, with the <style> element created in the SVG namespace, plus an
    opaque backdrop rect so the file is not white-on-white in a viewer with no
    page behind it. Pinned: "exported SVG carries the stylesheet".

(b) Colours defined with CSS custom properties did not survive the export at
    all (var(--hyperedge) resolves to black in a static SVG reader). Graph marks
    and their legend swatches now use literal colours. Pinned by the same check,
    which requires #f59e0b on .node-shape.kind-hyperedge inside the exported file.

(c) Long rule labels smeared across the canvas as unbreakable 170-character
    chains, overlapping each other and their neighbours. Rule labels are now cut
    to 34 characters on the canvas with an ellipsis, the full text stays in the
    details panel and the hover title. Pinned: "canvas labels are shortened".

(d) Overlapping labels everywhere: the app painted every label unconditionally.
    Placement is now a greedy pass in priority order (selection, vertices,
    hyperedges, rules, terms) with three candidate positions per label, marks and
    argument-position labels reserved as obstacles, and a label dropped rather
    than overlapped. Re-solved on every tick while the layout is warm. Pinned:
    "visible labels do not overlap each other" (0 of 81 boxes), "no placed label
    box sits on a mark box", "placement keeps most names visible" (81 placed,
    11 dropped).

(e) Hyperedges were indistinguishable when fitted: at 0.32x a 9 px diamond is
    3 px and the shapes read as noise. Marks now have an on-screen size floor
    (3.6 px) with a growth cap, and link stroke width is divided by the zoom.
    Pinned: "node marks stay at least 3px on screen when fitted" (3.60 px at
    0.32x), "hyperedge shapes are drawn, not hidden".

(f) The layout boiled: every arriving event reheated the force simulation, so
    settled nodes drifted tens of pixels per delta. An incremental update that
    adds nothing now only re-binds data; one that adds elements gets a short
    0.08-alpha nudge; a fresh view or a large change still gets a full layout.
    Pinned: "an incremental update keeps every node in place" (was 25.24 px, now
    0.71-1.17 px over ~98 nodes), "a new vertex does not shake the whole layout",
    "unrelated nodes keep their positions when one is added".

(g) The legend sat on top of the graph and swallowed drags and clicks meant for
    it. It is pointer-events: none now, and Fit measures its box and fits the
    graph beside it (fitView inset).

(h) The details panel was unusable on complex elements: a rule's 170-character
    label became the panel heading, and long terms stretched the sidebar. The
    heading is bounded to 120 characters with the full text on the title, table
    values are clamped with the whole value on hover, the table is
    table-layout: fixed with anywhere-wrapping, and a "Focus in graph" button
    zooms to the element at 1.6x. Pinned: "a rule's details heading is bounded",
    "the full rule text is available on the heading", "focus zooms in on the
    selected element" (1.6).

(i) A selected mark was a hairline at low zoom. Selection stroke is now
    scaled against the zoom (up to 9 px) so it stays visible at fit-to-view.

## 4. Graph-element churn: root cause and resolution

The round-1 report said rule pattern terms churn "because the constructor
registry grows". Measured, that is wrong. Two probes:

  probe A: fresh runtime, one digest, polls around two pack loads
    after order-sign      nodes=21 edges=13  -0/21 node items
    quiet order-sign      nodes= 0 edges=13  -21/0 node items   <-- all gone
    after arithmetic      nodes=28 edges=25  -0/28 node items
    quiet arithmetic      nodes= 0 edges=25  -28/0 node items   <-- all gone

  probe B: same, but a fresh GraphDigest for each reading
    order-sign  -0 removals,  arithmetic  -0 removals

So the loss was a function of reading the same graph twice through one digest,
not of loading packs. In visualiser/events.py the rule path returned its premise
and replacement objects on the cache-miss path only:

  if cached is not None:
      return cached, [], []        # no premises, no replacement

read() builds term nodes from the objects a rule returns, so the second read of
any rule-bearing graph dropped every premise term, and the third brought them
back. On a 25-rule graph that is ~90 node items removed and re-added per pair of
polls with no machine change at all. Measured before the fix: 21 removals after
the first pack and 28 after the second.

The objects that received unstable treatment were therefore the *premise and
replacement terms of a rule*, and only while the digest's rule cache was warm.
Their ids were never unstable — the identity caches also keyed on id() without
holding the object, which could hand a freed term's rendering to a different
term that reused the slot.

Resolution:
  - the rule cache stores the triple (item, premises, replacement) and returns
    it whole on a hit;
  - the text, anonymous-ordinal and rule-text caches hold the object alongside
    the value and are only trusted when the object is the same one;
  - nothing about the machine's state is hidden: an id that really leaves the
    graph still appears in items.removed.

After the fix, loading all 17 packs one at a time (17 loads, two polls each)
produced 0 removals and 0 spurious additions, 426 node items and 205 edges:

  $ PYTHONPATH=/home/user python -u probes/visualiser_churn_probe.py
  ... total removed across all pack loads: 0

Regression test: validation/test49_visualiser_churn.py, 6 sections:
  [1] second poll of an unchanged graph is silent: 4 events, then 0
  [2] second read keeps every premise of 13 rules, 23 node items unchanged
  [3] pack 2 added 12 edges and removed nothing; 23 existing ids unchanged
  [4] an id that really vanishes appears in items.removed
  [5] one added node produces exactly one node item and no removals
  [6] teardown
The test was shown to fail with the defect reintroduced:
  AssertionError: a poll with no machine change reported removals:
  ['t:0', 't:NonNegative(0)', 't:IsReal(0)', 't:[<?>, ?n]', ...]

## 5. Layout continuity and replay determinism

Asserted in the DOM suite against positions read from the drawn transform and
from the page's own placement record:

  an incremental update keeps every node in place        0.71px worst of 90
  a new vertex does not shake the whole layout           10.56px worst
  unrelated nodes keep their positions when one is added 9.96px over 14 nodes
  selection survives an unrelated update                 3 selected marks
  the details panel still describes the selection        continuity_probe
  seeking back to the same event rebuilds the same state ids identical
  the end of the recording is the full recorded graph    66 nodes
  replaying the same recording twice reaches the same state  66 nodes

